"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[356],{9356:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2016-02-08T15:42:00","modified":"2016-02-08T15:43:00","id":1025,"title":"Switching from Bower to NPM for Front End Dependencies","article":"<p>Today I have made the switch from using both NPM and Bower to just NPM in my web application projects for importing packages.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"why-did-we-use-both\\">Why did we use both?</h2>\\n<p>Traditionally NPM was good at the server side as it worked well with Node and is built into Node itself, so lots of packages are available there. However it was not good for the front-end i.e. web browsers, because when it brought in a package it then brought in the dependencies for each individual package you installed a copy as a sub folder of that package. Meaning if two packages required jQuery then you had two copies of jQuery, not ideal for passing over the internet to a user waiting for their page to load.</p>\\n<p>Bower got round this by flattening the dependencies into top level folders, rather than nesting them in sub folders for each package. This resulted in only single copies of the dependencies for the packages that the packages then shared. Of course there were some caveats to this but in general thats how it worked.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"so-why-change\\">So why change?</h2>\\n<p>Well NPM has recently hit version 3, a major change being its switch to a flatter approach similar to Bower, this makes it easier to manage in a similar way to what Bower does. And reducing the reliance on more than one build tool was appealing, due to increased productivity.</p>\\n<p>There is also the simple fact having two means two points of failure, relying on one means just handling one being unavailable, which doubles in probability if there are two rather than one.</p>\\n<p>Using the one system means I can be become better at just the single system, rather than splitting my knowledge between the 2. However it is still a good idea of at least knowing how other options work.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"any-issues\\">Any issues?</h2>\\n<p>Yes, the most prominent being alot of NPM packages are built for being used usually in NodeJS, so they use the CommonJS module pattern. In that pattern lazy loading modules are difficult to do and on the browser this can mean loading everything packaged together in a few giant files, regardless of whether it is needed or not, thats ok on a small application/site but on larger sites its definitely not practical. This is not true of all packages though as several implement the AMD pattern which allows lazy loading of modules, but it is something to be aware of.</p>\\n<p>Also NPM demands a package.json file making pulling in assets that are not built for NPM, maybe directly from GitHub, difficult to do. Whereas Bower didnt have this restriction.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"is-it-worth-it\\">Is it worth it?</h2>\\n<p>Personally I feel it is, controlling a single package manager rather than two feels more natural, also it means you can be more of an expert in one rather than splitting your knowledge across two package managers consistently. Plus NPM has the idea of dependencies and devDependencies which works well in separating the build tools required from the front end tools required to run your application.</p>\\n<p>All of which sort of negates the need to have Bower.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["javascript","npm","bower"]}')}}]);